{
  "behaviorChanges": [
    "`behavior-spec-status` accepts `--base <ref>` and reports what the branch changed since the merge base with that ref. Two counts and a list: `behavior_specs_added_inactive: <N> (vs <ref>)` counts EVERY newly inactive scenario (annotated or not -- a compliant `@pending` is still growth); `behavior_specs_added_issues: <N> (vs <ref>)` counts the scenarios the branch left without the owner and un-pend-trigger annotations, and one `behavior_specs_added_issue:` line per such scenario cites the canonical form. Those two are DIFFERENT sets, not nested: a branch that strips metadata from a scenario that already existed reports 0 added and 1 issue. When `behaviorSpecs.pendingRequiresOwnerAndTrigger` is false the issue half prints `disabled by adapter` and the count still reports growth. The comparison reads two committed trees, never the worktree, and BOTH counts print `unknown` -- never `0` -- when a ref, merge base, feature file or path scope cannot be read, or when the report fails unexpectedly. REPORTING ONLY: it never changes the exit code, which stays repo-wide issues under --strict or strict enforcement. Without `--base` there is no new output and no git work at all.",
    "`behavior_specs_instruction:` gains a trailing clause naming the machine-checkable annotation form: \" Canonical form: `@pending @owner:<goal-or-lane> @reason:<why> @unpend:<trigger>`.\" It is interpolated from the same constant the refusals use, so the two cannot drift. Every `behavior_specs_issue:` line is byte-identical to 0.52.0 -- those are the lines consumers parse.",
    "KNOWN GAP, stated rather than claimed fixed: a Gherkin scenario has no stable identity, so same-named scenarios in one feature file are paired in line order. Inserting a new bare `@pending` ABOVE an existing same-named one and annotating the original makes the new instance take the old one's slot -- growth is still counted, but `behavior_specs_added_issues` attributes it to the pre-existing scenario and reports 0. Deferred deliberately: the pairing helper is shared with `behavior-spec-delta-check`, and three of four review rounds in 0.50.0 showed every patch to it minted a fresh false refusal on that gate. No exit code is affected. Pinned by a test documented to fail when the successor plan closes it.",
    "`behavior-spec-status` and `methodology-status` now compute the repo-wide behavior-spec record ONCE per invocation instead of twice (it walks and parses every in-scope .feature file). Output is unchanged. `methodology-status` deliberately carries no delta line: no base ref is resolved in its scope, so a delta there would mean adding a merge-base plus a unified diff to a verb policy 15 requires before every plan finalization, merge and delivery. `behavior-spec-status --base` is the supported delta surface."
  ],
